<blockquote class="uncited"><div>You can assemble these constraints with the position constraint and build a 5x5 K matrix. On the other hand, you could just treat them one-by-one, which is simpler because you don't need to invert a 5x5 matrix.</div></blockquote>It is simpler, but using the 5x5 system would be equivalent (w.r.t. to your sequential impulses) to a block Gauss-Seidel which has superior convergence, *especially* for joints where the angular term is dominant. <br>From a practical standpoint and also having SIMD in head I would suggest solving blocks of 3x3 systems, that is simply solving the linear and angular terms...<br><br>Cheers,<br>-Dirk<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=14">Dirk Gregorius</a> — Wed Aug 23, 2006 5:49 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
