about building the constraints by starting with the position-level constraint: if you look at Erin's post (and if i understand correctly) he starts with the equations for momentum/velocity and not position.. and if i didn't totally misunderstand his 2006 presentation, the normal/tangent constraints are likewise specified by directly using velocity (i.e constraining the relative velocity to be 0, instead of constraining the first derivative of the relative position to be 0).<br><br><br>also, could you possibly explain how: K = J * W * JT <br>relates to: K = 1/Ma + 1/Mb - [~ra]Ia^-1[~ra] - [~rb]Ib^-1[~rb]<br><br>if W == Inverse mass matrix, then is what i'm calling K equivalent to your W? Or are our Ks identical?<br><br>if the latter, what does W (i.e the inverse mass matrix _before_ being transformed/projected/whatever onto the jacobian) look like? <br><br>if the former, am i simply doing the J * W * JT without knowing it? is the whole process of transforming the inverse inertia tensors by the skew matrices equivalent to J * W * JT?<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=748">raigan2</a> — Wed Aug 23, 2006 3:47 pm</p><hr />
